  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "s1",
      "kind": "abstract",
      "title": "Summary",
      "text": "We report two siblings with features suggestive of Kabuki syndrome in whom exome sequencing identified a novel heterozygous KMT2D variant. We describe their clinical course and compare it with previously reported cases."
    },
    {
      "id": "s2",
      "kind": "case",
      "title": "Patient 1",
      "text": "The proband, a 6-year-old girl, was referred to our clinic for global developmental delay and short stature. She was born at term after an uneventful pregnancy. On examination she had long palpebral fissures with eversion of the lateral third of the lower eyelids, arched eyebrows and prominent ear lobes. Persistent fetal fingertip pads were noted on both hands.\n\nEchocardiography revealed coarctation of the aorta, which was repaired at the age of 4 months. She had recurrent otitis media during infancy. Her hearing was normal on repeated audiological testing, and brain MRI was unremarkable. At 5 years, formal assessment confirmed marked developmental delay."
    },
    {
      "id": "s3",
      "kind": "case",
      "title": "Patient 2",
      "text": "Her younger brother, aged 3 years, presented with hypotonia and feeding difficulties in the neonatal period. He had a cleft palate, which was surgically repaired at 11 months. Mild intellectual disability was suspected at his last assessment. Their mother reported frequent infections during her own childhood."
    },
    {
      "id": "s4",
      "kind": "section",
      "title": "Discussion",
      "text": "Both siblings share the characteristic facial gestalt, although the proband shows a more severe neurodevelopmental profile. Segregation analysis showed that the variant was inherited from the mother, who had a milder phenotype."
    }
  ],
